WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.480
What would a British Christmas dinner
be without roast turkey?

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:05.080
Well it would still
be Christmas dinner.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.480
Turkey's dominance of our festive
table is comparatively recent,

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:11.880
and for much of the last 500 years,
beef was the meat of choice.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:13.920
Turkeys are native
to central America

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.680
where they were domesticated
around 200BC

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:18.760
By the time Columbus
and his fellow explorers

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.120
started to discover the
delights of indigenous foods

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.080
they were eaten far beyond
their original pecking grounds,

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:26.480
and had spread to North America
and the Caribbean.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:29.040
From there, the spread
of turkey was spectacular.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.720
By 1511, every ship leaving
the Americas for Spain

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.440
was ordered to bring back
five breeding pairs.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.280
By the 1530s, they were
being farmed in France.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:40.400
And in England in 1541
they were restricted by law,

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.600
along with cranes and swans, to only
one such bird being served per feast.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:47.800
It's not clear exactly where
the British got turkeys from.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:49.440
They were imported from America,

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.040
but they also came in via Turkey,
hence the name.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:56.160
They were large, versatile and most
importantly visually impressive.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:58.480
After all, another competitor
was the peacock.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:00.520
Turkeys apparently taste better.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.680
A popular way to serve them
was in a raised pie

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:05.160
with the tail, wings and head
and neck cooked onto skewers

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:06.600
and put back in the pie.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:09.320
Resplendently gazing back
at the would-be diner.

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:11.160
Turkeys were not just for Christmas,

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:12.840
they were eaten
throughout the winter.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:15.520
Fowl was at its best
from September to March.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.800
Inevitably a big, juicy,
expensive bird

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:20.360
in season in December became
associated with the feasting

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:23.000
which went on for 12 days
from the 25th.

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:24.080
By the 17th Century

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.480
turkey was well established
as one of the key Christmas meats.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:30.480
In America, the early settlers
were saved from starvation

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:32.840
by Native Americans
who shared their food.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:36.320
In 1621, the first recorded
Thanksgiving feast took place.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:37.200
Turkey was eaten,

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.440
and it became an established part of
the various Thanksgiving celebrations

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:42.400
which were held over
the next 200 years.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.280
Later, Thanksgiving was declared
a national holiday

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.080
with turkey at centre stage.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:48.960
By the 19th Century in Britain,

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:50.960
turkey was very much
still a Christmas dish,

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:52.480
but not the main one.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:54.320
If you were rich, you had
lots of other meats,

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:55.920
and beef was still
the mainstay.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.040
If you were poor,
turkey was unaffordable.

00:01:58.120 --> 00:01:59.760
You might have
a cheaper goose instead.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:01.680
That said, in 1861,

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:04.440
the quintessential middle-class
writer and sometime plagiarist,

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:06.320
Isabella Beeton, declared that...

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:15.800
Things didn’t really change until the
second half of the 20th Century when,

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:17.800
after two world wars
and a depression,

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.200
meals were rather smaller,
even for the rich,

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.800
and feasts centred
on one meaty roast.

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:26.160
In the 1950s, America cast a
glamourous pall over British food,

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:29.680
and as we greedily embraced images
of golden Thanksgiving turkeys,

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.680
we turned to them more and more
for our own festive fare.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:35.480
Even if your oven was
too small, help was at hand.

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:38.440
Well into the 60s, the local bakers
would open on Christmas morning,

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:41.080
their ovens ready
for the locals' Christmas roast.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.200
Now our image of the ideal Christmas
dinner is focussed on the turkey,

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:47.000
but there are voices of dissent...

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:51.840
They whisper.
And they may have a point.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:54.960
After all, turkey can
apparently be a lovely thing,

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:58.200
but if we really liked it wouldn’t we
cook it more than once a year?

